 "prose": [
  {
   "title": null,
   "text": "Form manages the collective state of its FormFields. There are two primary ways of managing Form state, each of which is outline below.",
   "format": "md"
  },
  {
   "title": "controlled",
   "text": "A controlled Form is similar to a controlled input element in React. The value state is held by the caller and connected to the Form via `value` and `onChange`.\n\nstorybook",
   "format": "md"
  },
  {
   "title": "uncontrolled",
   "text": "An uncontrolled Form is similar to an uncontrolled input element in React. The value state is held by the Form.\n\nstorybook",
   "format": "md"
  },
  {
   "title": "React/DOM Properties",
   "text": "At its core, the Form component is a regular <form> element. Thus, both DOM and React properties, methods, and events are accessible. To read up on all of the possible DOM attributes and types available for form elements, check out this [MDN Web Documents page.](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element/form) To learn more about DOM events and methods, you can read more on the [MDN Web Events documentation page](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Events).\n\nAlso, feel free to read about the [types of React events available](https://reactjs.org/docs/handling-events.html), or see [how DOM attributes change in React.](https://reactjs.org/docs/dom-elements.html) Working in tandem with Styled Components, you also have access to the [as property.](https://styled-components.com/docs/api#as-polymorphic-prop)",
   "format": "md"
  }
 ],
